A weak import that binds to NULL now says so.

A weak undefined symbol does not stop the loader. It binds to NULL, the process runs, and the first
call through it faults at 0x0 with nothing attached to say which symbol it was. There are four
places here where that happens and all four returned 0 in silence: flat namespace, weak, two level,
and the case where the dependent dylib itself is not loaded, which NULLs every symbol from it at
once.

Each now logs under the loader existing switch, DYLD_PRINT_WEAK_BINDINGS, rather than a new one.
The line names the symbol, the image that wanted it and which of the four paths it took, so a 0x0
fault can be matched against a list of names instead of guessed at.

Task #238 counted 66 such symbols across 454 Mach-O files and then could not say whether any
application reaches one, because there was no way to ask. This is the way to ask.

The default is unchanged: the flag is off unless the variable is set, and the returned value is
still 0. Binding a stub instead of NULL would break weak linking, whose entire purpose is that the
address compares equal to zero.

